#%PAM-1.0
#
# PAM stack for the vela lock screen.
#
# vela points PAM at this directory with pam_start_confdir(3) instead of
# installing a file under /etc/pam.d, so the lock works from a plain `stow`
# and never needs root. Authentication still runs against the system
# databases: pam_unix reaches /etc/shadow through the setuid unix_chkpwd
# helper, which works fine for an unprivileged process.
#
# PASSWORD ONLY, ON PURPOSE. This used to include /etc/pam.d/system-auth, and
# on Fedora that stack runs pam_fprintd before pam_unix. On a machine with an
# enrolled fingerprint reader pam_fprintd waits out its own timeout before the
# password is even asked for, the lock's watchdog fired first, and three of
# those "could not judge" results released the lock by design -- so anyone at
# the keyboard could unlock it by pressing Enter three times and waiting.
# pam_faillock is left out for the same reason: a locked-out account answers
# with an error rather than a failure. pam_unix only ever says yes or no.
#
# (caelestia's shell does the same, and runs fingerprint as a separate stack.)

auth        required    pam_unix.so
account     required    pam_unix.so
